 eu is china's first trading partner  on average we trade over 1 billion euros  a day  and to it can energize our economic  recovery  but we want more fairness  we want a more balanced relationship  that  also means reciprocity and  a level playing field that's why we  welcome to the signature  of the agreement on geographical  indications it's a big step  in the right direction we are working  on a comprehensive investment agreement  and concrete results  in other important areas ursula will say  more about that and in the digital  domain  we defend our vision of a free  open and secure cyberspace  for the good of our people and of our  societies  as global players the eu  and china have global responsibilities  this means upholding the rules-based  international order  the national security law for hong kong  continues to raise grave concerns  the eu and our member states have  responded  with one clear voice democratic voices  in hong kong should be hurt rights  protected and autonomy preserved  we called on china to keep their  promises to the people of hong kong  and the international community we  reiterated  our concerns over china's treatment of  minorities  in xinjiang and tibet and the treatment  of human rights defenders and  journalists  we asked for access for independent  observers  to changing and we called for the  release  of the arbitrarily detained swedish  citizen minhai and two canadian citizens  we agreed to discuss these issues in  details at the human rights dialogue  in being later this year which will also  include  we hope a field visit to tibet  we called on china to refrain from  unilateral actions in the south china  sea  to respect international law and avoid  escalations  profit 19 we miss a deep and  urgent threat only collective  and transparent action will send this  virus to the history books  there is only one way to find a vaccine  and deploy it in all countries  and that's global cooperation  we expect all countries to cooperate  with the impartial  independent and comprehensive evaluation  of the international health response to  javit 19  and support the who to identify  the source of the virus we entered  china to pursue an economic recovery  that leads  to structural reforms and it shapes a  greener  more sustainable economy and this  includes  implementing the g20 action plan to  drive sustainable global growth  and reduce global tensions and  in africa china should be engaged  in multilateral efforts on debt relief  that will spur  economic recovery in conclusion  no distribution  extremely important  [Music]  and now we're going to switch to berlin  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  is  passing the floor now to madam president  please yes thank you so much indeed it  was a frank an open  and a constructive and intense uh  quattro log that we had today i was  about to say dialogue but  this is not the case so  and i it was a broad spectrum of topics  indeed from human rights to climate to  digital  to trade and i want to focus on the  trade  part indeed as we committed to at the  summit in june  we have stepped up our engagement at the  political level  over the past weeks notably in the areas  of trade  climate and digital and  here i want to start with in the trader  agenda with the comprehensive  agreement on investment  last time last summer two months ago i  stressed to president xi that the  european union  remains committed to a genuine  result-oriented  engagement and progress we need concrete  results  and now we have agreement amongst  negotiation negotiations on three  important issues  first of all on the disciplines  regarding the behavior of  state-owned enterprises then on forced  technology transfer and on  transparency on subsidies this is an  important part an important step forward  however i want to caution that  a lot a lot still remains to be done in  other important and difficult chapters  of the agreement  particularly into two areas the one is  market access  and the other one is sustainable  development  we expect that the market access  barriers  in china will be removed especially  for future and emerging digital  technologies they are the core of our  economies it's telecommunication it's  computer sectors  it's health biotechnology and the new  energy vehicles and transport  we see that our investors just face too  many barriers in these key  sectors and for us with  market access it's not a question of  meeting halfway  but it's a question of rebalancing the  asymmetry and  a question of openness of our respective  markets  so we need china to move on these two  issues and  we needed to move if we are to achieve  to achieve our shared objective of  finalizing negotiations  this year so in other words china has to  convince  us that it is worth having an investment  agreement  the second topic wasn't is a chinese  over capacity  it's also a serious issue for us both in  traditional sectors like steel and  aluminium  but also in high tech there are so far  unfortunately no concrete steps forward  so this needs to change and we addressed  it very clearly  joining the global forum on steel access  capacity and  eliminating the state subsidies that  contribute to this over capacity  would be necessary steps in this regard  and i've agreed with president xi to  follow up on this  bilaterally we also  reiterated to president xi that china  had  committed to extending to eu businesses  the favorable treatment granted to  us operators under the u.s china trade  deal agreed in  january and we expect this commitment  to be met now what the digital is  concerned  first of all the european union welcomes  that  china reaffirmed its commitment to a  human-centric approach to ai  and welcomes that work on this will be  carried forward  in the g20 here too we hope  to see concrete progress in this context  we reaffirmed the importance of market  access also here for goods and services  in the area of telecommunications  the european market is open and european  companies must have  fair and equal access to the chinese  market  in return we took note of president xi's  commitment  to look into these matters  at the same time the european union will  continue to stress  the importance of its networks being  secure  and in this overall context of all these  different  topics concerning the digital agenda we  have together  i very much welcome that we had the  first ever  high-level digital dialogue which took  place last week  so we agreed with president xi that we  will continue and broaden this dialogue  what the  scope and the topics are concerned  finally on the wto reform  china has repeatedly stated that it is  committed  to the wto that also requires that china  negotiates to strengthen existing rules  on industrial subsidies in the context  of the world trade  organization's reform and in line  with the summit commitment it made in  2019 this was a topic too  last but not least we had a brief  exchange  on corona virus pandemic the pandemic  we're all facing we discussed the need  to continue to act in global solidarity  and cooperation in this matter  it is very important to engage in the  global framework  not only to find a vaccine but also to  make it accessible to low and medium  income countries  as you know we've set up a whole global  framework  to ensure that and we invited china to  join  this covex facility it is important  also to continue understanding the  origins of the pandemic and therefore  we were very clear that it is very  important to give  the who all the possibilities to have  to lead its investigations on the origin  of covet 19 to be  successful in summer very concrete  meeting  with tangible actions discussed  thank you madam president for your  remarks we'll uh now take  a couple of questions here in brussels  first and since this is a virtual press  conference please  please bear with us may i ask you to  stick to one  question for journalists and clearly  state um  to whom you want to address your  question to i'm going to start first  with marcus price from aarde marcus can  you hear us  yes i can yeah yumi as well yes please  ask your question oh my question goes to  chancellor merkel  and so if i may i would like to ask in  uh in german um  thank you  next question is for stuart lau  stuart can you hear us  yes thank you very much um i also have a  question for madam chancellor your  supporters call you the western leader  who knows chinese leadership thinking  the best  but on the other hand your critics say  germany's strong trading interest  empowers china not just economically but  also geopolitically  and it is now competing with european  technology and promoting political views  alternative to western values looking  back at the  last 15 years and looking at china today  do you think your approach succeeded or  would you have done things differently  thank you  [Music]  [Music]  thank you staying in berlin for a next  round of questions  passing flow to my colleagues  um  [Music]  uh  lanka continuing further on in berlin  andreas from reuters  [Music]  thank you we're continuing the press  conference here in brussels and i'm  going to take two more questions  i'm first going to start with angela  mauro from the huffington post  angela can you hear us yes i can hear  you can you hear me  yes please okay i um  hello and thanks for the opportunity i  have a question for chancellor merkel  if she can elaborate more on the climate  part of the deal  uh did you discuss a hypothetical  deadline for china to become a neutral  country  for the emissions i mean for the eu is  2050.  before the meeting they told us that was  this hypothetical deadline  uh on 2016 for china to respect  these goals is this uh realistic like  the line  thank you  thank you we're going to go to  madame president again because i forgot  you  your question was addressed to you as  well on the investment agreement  and you wanted to add a couple of  remarks i suppose yeah well  uh indeed um so what the investment  agreement is concerned and the timeline  of potentially  having it as an agreement that we can  sign at the end of the year  uh indeed um we started  well let's let's put it the other way  when we had the summit in  june uh 2020 up to then  almost no progress was seen and  the negotiations team only did not come  on a high political level  so we were very clear in june  that there would be no investment  agreement if we do not see substantial  progress and we have seen a reaction  that is positive  so now it's a high level negotiation  team  that is sitting on that topic  and we've seen the progress i told you  about  the fields where we are advancing now  but i must say there is still a lot to  do  so on the market access for example the  telecom and computer services  there are these famous joint venture and  equity caps  restrictions that have to be lifted same  goes for the automotive sector for  example  that here also the economic needs tests  need to be lifted just to name two but  it shows to you  that we are really serious about having  access to the chinese market and to tear  down  the barriers same goes for the uh  sustainable development so  we we need here progress of course on  the chinese side there are also demands  but as i've said it is now uh the time  to prove  that there's a real interest to step up  to change  things for the better then there is the  possibility to have this  investment agreement so it's not a  matter of time but it's a matter of  substance now  thank you madam president going to take  a last question now from david carretta  radio radically david  italian  [Music]  is  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  the situation the perimeter  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  and with these final remarks this  concludes our press conference  thank you all for joining and see you  soon  [Music]  you

